Why'd you leave out the (IMNSHO sensible) proposition I made?  Namely:
producing excessive melanin in an environment where it is not needed is
maladaptive, since doing so burdens the energy and resources of the body
unnecessarily.  This is likely part of the reason we don't have big
canines, or (in most cases) much body hair.*  It's unnecessary.  Certainly
there's nothing, on the surface, maladaptive in having large rending
teeth, but when you consider that it takes more to grow them, it becomes
pretty clear that it's a waste.  Part of my longterm gripe with straight
Darwinism is that it does not account for such factors (the blind cave
fish example could never be adequately explained by this model.)  Only
when you take into account that unnecessary organs and other bodily
structures and processes are wasteful, does it make much sense.  I was
certainly glad to find this (to me) patently obvious observation was well
supported when I got into college!  I remember being roundly ridiculed by
my (asshole) biology prof in high school for daring to suggest such a
think.  I don't think the guy had read any new science since he graduated
in the '40s!  >:)  

[* Yes I know there are varying theories, and combinations thereof, for
explaining reduction in human body hair; I favour a combination of
several, otherwise it is just too simplistic, Occam not withstanding.]

> About dairy-farming I dunno.  It's certainly possible that both explanations
