Not necessarily.  The body has finite resources, and there are much more
important adaptations that the desert dweller has to make.  Also, Central
and No. Europeans became far more dependent on dairying than Middle
Easterners.  And one other factor I'd been thinking of several years ago
(in connection with something else entirely) that may be relevant here:
"white" people seem to have the widest variety of phenotypal differences
in the world (besides height and a few other things).  More differing hair
colours, eye colours, weights, etc (the record heaviest and lightest
people were both "white", and in fact so were the tallest and shortest,
though Africans have the largest whole-culture variation in that aspect,
from pygmy to...Watusi? I forget the tall ones.  One other factor in this
variation bit may be that "white" is a bogus category, biologically. There
are a LOT of differences between Celtic, Nordic, Slavic, etc. people, and
the non-Indo-Europeans, like the Basque and Lappland Finns, are as
different from say a German as Arab is from a Frenchman.  Still (to get to
the point) it may just be that "white" humans are less stable genetically
and mutate more often than Middle Easterners.  Yes this is WILD speculation.

> Don't be surprised if it isn't; Europeans may well have been
> dairying longer than the historical Middle Eastern cultures (Stonehenge is
> older than the Pyramids, you know).

Survey says: [X] [X] [X] !  The 1st (known) section of Stonehenge was
built ~1900-1600 bce, about 1000 years AFTER the pyramids.  [Hawkins, refs.
below].  Other more optimistic researchers put the date for Stonehenge I at
around 2800-2300 bce, right around the time of the pyramids (~2660+)
[Whitehouse]. Herding of cattle in Britain appears to date from ca. 3000
bce, true, but considering that these 1st (known) agriculturalists in
Britain were migratory people from the east Mediterranean, and archaelogy
indicates that they brought herding WITH them, odds are it started in the
Mideast.  Anyway, I think you were thinking of New Grange in Ireland which
does indeed date to somewhere between 4700 and 2200 bce (according to RC
dating, and other methods -- estimates vary wildly; the Irish govt tends to
round the figure off to "5000 years ago" but that doesn't mean much.  At
any rate, cattle rearing in Ireland dates to the Neolithic (pollen dating
suggests 3200 bce).  However cattle, goats, horses and pigs are not
native to the isles, and so must have been imported by the migrants, most
of whom again appear to have originated in the Mediterranean region (later
populations were from north-coastal Western Europe, e.g. Brittany, France,
Iberia, Germany. [O'Kelly, Hawkins, Whitehouse].  All in all the evidence 
points at the Near and Middle East for the development of European
agriculture.  I don't have the refs to check out the Scandinavian
patterns, but I suspect they are similar.

I'd personally put the lactose tolerance of most Celto-Teutonics down to
the later heavy dependence on dairy farming evinced by the Gauls and
Nordics, from the Hallstatt period onward. I don't think it would take
long to adapt to such a diet (1000 years or so tops).  I may be wrong
on that one though...

Anyway, like I said, I find it highly far-fetched that European dairying
began as a response to not enough vitamin D.  Much more likely to me is
that it began in the Mideast as the evidence suggests, and in response to
a harsh climate where ANY food must be take for what it's worth.  This
pattern of usage is quite common in subsistence cultures (such as the
Eskimo, and the !Kung -- even the fluids from misc. organs is cherished,
and almost nothing is wasted.)  When it spread to Europe, it simple became
a way of life that hasn't changed much, mainly due to economics,
efficiency, and terrain in many cases unsuited for heavy crop growing, but
great for livestock.

