Date: Mon, 14 Jun 93 14:49:54 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: SPACE/FREE: Governments in Space 

Perry writes:
>I think you miss the major benefit of space migration: being able to
>go to unclaimed territory. There is no place left on earth where a
>state has not claimed the right to rule you -- on the other hand,
>history has shown that it is hard to rule the frontier, and the
>frontier in this case is infinitely larger than in the past.

States have often (usually?) claimed the right to rule over various
frontiers in the past.  It has only been hard to rule various
frontiers because of historically contingent facts about the economics
of war and policing.  Previous frontiers have often been large areas
easy to hide in and expensive and dangerous to move armies across.
The technology of war in the inner solar system may simply not be
analogous to these previous frontiers.  

Robin Hanson


