sign that the toxins are building up and I back off.  (No pain, longer
life. :-)  The beta carotene, E, C, chromium, etc. look
good.   The rest of the regimen looks interesting;
can you describe the purpose of Ferulic Acid and DMG?
The point about diversity is quite good; I alternate between
several different brands of multivitamins.

I keep around beer & wine and sometimes go for one or two beers
or glasses of wine a day. I try not to get drunk; eg I avoid
the keg at parties.  (Just being a paranoid party-pooper, of course. :-)  
I take a supplement of polyphenols, which are the much-praised
antioxidants in green tea and read wine.  Not regularly checking
in with a doctor is indeed foolish, but I must admit to a similar 
hang-up.  Part of the problem is I can't find a good doctor, ie one
that can talk knowledgeably about life extension, is willing
to give regular blood tests without a physical checkup, etc.
I just joined the Life Extension Foundation and am looking forward
to their list.

> MAY EQUATION (sketchy form):

> Extra Years of Quality Life = (Quality of Life Term x Extra Years) +
>                               (Probability of Reanimation x Extra
>                               Years after Reanimation x Quality of
>                               Life after Reanimation)

This is a good start, but misses some important factors.    One
that immediately makes a huge difference is 

+ P(Uploading) x  Extra Years x Quality of Life after Uploading

Also note that P(Reanimation) depends on lifespan (somebody suspended
today has a better chance than somebody suspended in 1980, etc.) and
and P(Uploading) highly depends on the two previous factors.  Therefore,
if P(Reanimation) and P(Uploading) are at all significant, we get 
_tremendous_ leverage from adding even a few extra years of human life.
That's why I'm so "paranoid" about my diet, lifestyle, etc.  

Let's plug in some quite conservative numbers.  Take my human
lifespan to be 90 years, deanimation in 2054.  Take P(Reanimation) of 
a 2054 suspension to be 50%.  Take a suspension of 200 years, and a 
lifespan after reanimation of 200 years.  Take P(Uploading|Reanimation) 
in 2454 to be 50%.  Take lifespan after uploading of 1 billion years.  
This gives us 
	90+(.50)*200+(.25)*(1e9) = 250 million years

The two addends from the May Equation aren't even significant in the
result, but they are overwhelmingly important in determining 
P(Reanimation) and P(Uploading).  Also you see why Extropians are
so concerned about the very long-range future, about what is
theoretically possible in terms of deanimation, reanimation,
uploading, etc. even if it's well beyond today's technology.
Getting even order-of-magnitude estimates of these probabilities 
makes a huge difference in our life expectancies.

We need an objective measurement for "Quality".  I suggest computational
complexity.  For example Perry's Jupiter-sized brain, with
a computational complexity 1 trillion times the human brain and a life
expectancy of 1 billion years, gives overall 1e21 human-quality
years.

Some may choose to discount reanimation or uploaded years (1 extra year 
in a 500 year lifespan is not worth as much as 1 extra year in a 90
year lifespan).  On the other hand, if we factor in the quality
of life, living through the year 2500 as a transhuman or
900 million as a posthuman will be worth more than living
through 1993 as a human.

Current overall population lifespan trends don't mean too much, since
(1) only a small fraction of people have followed a good
life extension regimen (CR/FR/PR, antioxidants, etc.) from
an early age, and (2) the two biggest components in the upgraded
May equation (cryonics reanimation and uploading) don't show
up in current statistics.  Nevertheless, we should track
the centenarian statistics closely over the next few decades
to see what impact improved medicine, healthier living practices,
etc. will have on lifespan, since that greatly effects P(Reanimation)
and thereby P(Uploading).

